                  description. The approach has several advantages over
                  traditional ways to investigate intonation, such as the
                  applicability to spontaneous speech, language- and
                  domain-independency, and the potential of revealing meaningful
                  functions of intonation. These features make the approach
                  particularly useful for language documentation, where the
                  description of prosody is often lacking. The core of this
                  approach is a cluster analysis on a time-series of f0
                  measurements and consists of two scripts (Praat and R,
                  available
                  fromhttps://constantijnkaland.github.io/contourclustering/).
                  Graphical user interfaces can be used to perform the analyses
                  on collected data ranging from spontaneous to highly
                  controlled speech. There is limited need for manual annotation
                  prior to analysis and speaker variability can be accounted
                  for. After cluster analysis, Praat textgrids can be generated
